items.doc:
the owned pointer. keeps its allocator, so it frees with the one that allocated.
@owns("ptr"): box is used like a T&, and when it goes out of scope *ptr is deleted first,
then box's own delete (below) frees the memory. Any library can make such a type

A map ordered by key. Lookups are a binary search.
ponytail: sorted arrays, so put and remove move the entries after the key (O(n));
a B-tree if maps with many thousands of keys change often

A lock around a value: lock() waits for it and gives a guard, through which the value is reached;
deleting the guard unlocks it. Don't move a mutex while it's locked (share it: shared<mutex<T>>,
or borrow it from a thread whose handle goes first).
